  <h1>Design a Digital Wallet System</h1>
  <p class=3D"sub">Add money, pay, send and withdraw. Every rupee is credit=
ed and debited exactly once, the balance never goes negative, and the books=
 always balance, even when servers crash halfway.</p>
  <div class=3D"meta"><span class=3D"tag-red">High Priority</span><span cla=
ss=3D"tag-amber">=E2=97=8F Hard</span><span>=E2=8F=B1 50 min read</span><sp=
an>=F0=9F=94=8C 7 APIs</span><span>=F0=9F=97=84 8 tables</span><span>=F0=9F=
=A7=AD 12 diagrams</span><span>=F0=9F=94=8D 13 deep dives</span></div>
  <div class=3D"whatis">
    <div class=3D"wi-text">
      <h4>=F0=9F=91=9B What is a Digital Wallet?</h4>
      <p>A digital wallet (Paytm Wallet, PhonePe Wallet, Amazon Pay balance=
, PayPal balance) is an account that <strong>holds money for a user inside =
our system</strong>. Users load it from a card or bank, spend it at merchan=
ts, send it to friends and withdraw it back to their bank.</p>
      <p>The hard part isn't the UI. It's the <strong>money</strong>: two p=
ayments hitting the same wallet at the same millisecond, a client that retr=
ies a "send =E2=82=B9500" request, a transfer between two databases where o=
ne side crashes, and a bank that says "timeout" after it actually sent the =
money.</p>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"wmock" aria-hidden=3D"true">
      <div class=3D"wcard"><div class=3D"wl">Wallet balance</div><div class=
=3D"wb">=E2=82=B92,450<small>.00</small></div><div class=3D"wsub">Available=
 =E2=82=B92,150 =C2=B7 =E2=82=B9300 on hold</div>
        <div class=3D"wbtns"><span>=EF=BC=8B Add</span><span>=E2=86=97 Send=
</span><span>=E2=87=A9 Withdraw</span></div></div>
      <div class=3D"wtx">
        <div><span class=3D"ic up">=EF=BC=8B</span><span class=3D"d">Added =
from bank card<small>Today, 10:02</small></span><b class=3D"cr">+=E2=82=B95=
00.00</b></div>
        <div><span class=3D"ic">=E2=98=95</span><span class=3D"d">Paid to C=
orner Cafe<small>Today, 09:41</small></span><b>=E2=88=92=E2=82=B9120.00</b>=
</div>
        <div><span class=3D"ic">=E2=86=97</span><span class=3D"d">Sent to R=
avi<small>Yesterday</small></span><b>=E2=88=92=E2=82=B9300.00</b></div>
        <div><span class=3D"ic hold">=E2=8F=B3</span><span class=3D"d">With=
drawal to bank<small>Processing</small></span><b class=3D"hd">=E2=82=B9300.=
00</b></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class=3D"lead" style=3D"margin-top:22px">In this chapter we design the=
 wallet end to end: the ledger, the locking, idempotency, transfers across =
database shards (and whether that needs <strong>two-phase commit</strong>),=
 and how money moves safely in and out through payment gateways and banks.<=
/p>
  <div class=3D"hard">
    <div><b>=F0=9F=94=92 No double spend</b><p>Two debits race for the same=
 =E2=82=B9500. One conditional UPDATE under a row lock decides.</p></div>
    <div><b>=F0=9F=94=81 Exactly-once money</b><p>Idempotency keys stored i=
n the same transaction as the money movement.</p></div>
    <div><b>=F0=9F=A7=BE Books always balance</b><p>Double-entry ledger: ev=
ery transaction's debits equal its credits, across shards too.</p></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"questions-to-ask-the-interviewer" data-toc=
=3D"1. Clarifying Questions"><span class=3D"sec-n">1.</span> Questions to A=
sk the Interviewer</h2>
<div class=3D"cards"><p class=3D"lead">Pin down what money can do, what "ne=
ver wrong" means, and which systems outside ours touch the money, before dr=
awing anything.</p>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"which-money-movements-are-in-scope" data-toc=
=3D"1. Which money movements are in scope?"><span class=3D"qn">Q1</span>Whi=
ch money movements are in scope?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"q=
a-l">Why ask</span><p>Each movement has a different counterparty. Wallet-to=
-wallet moves stay inside our database; add-money and withdraw talk to a pa=
yment gateway or bank that can fail, time out or answer late.</p></div><div=
 class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>Add mone=
y (top-up) from card / UPI / netbanking, pay a merchant, send to another us=
er (P2P), withdraw to a bank account, and refunds from merchants. Also show=
 balance and transaction history. Credit lines, cashback rules and multi-cu=
rrency are out of scope.</p></div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"single-currency-how-do-we-store-amounts" data=
-toc=3D"2. Single currency? How do we store amounts?"><span class=3D"qn">Q2=
</span>Single currency? How do we store amounts?</h3><div class=3D"qa why">=
<span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>Multiple currencies bring FX rates an=
d conversion entries. Floating point loses paise (0.1 + 0.2 =E2=89=A0 0.3).=
</p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</sp=
an><p>INR only. Store every amount as an integer number of <strong>paise</s=
trong> (<code>BIGINT</code>): =E2=82=B9500.00 =3D <code>50000</code>.</p></=
div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"what-is-the-expected-scale" data-toc=3D"3. Wh=
at is the expected scale?"><span class=3D"qn">Q3</span>What is the expected=
 scale?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>Dec=
ides whether one database is enough or we must shard, and how hot a single =
wallet can get.</p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Intervi=
ewer answer</span><p>100M wallets, 20M daily active users, about 50M money =
movements a day. Festival sales and salary days bring 10=C3=97 spikes.</p><=
/div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"can-a-balance-ever-go-negative" data-toc=3D"4=
. Can a balance ever go negative?"><span class=3D"qn">Q4</span>Can a balanc=
e ever go negative?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask<=
/span><p>If overdraft is allowed we need credit limits and collections. If =
not, every debit needs a check that is safe under concurrency. <span class=
=3D"pill our">our addition</span></p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span clas=
s=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>Never. No overdraft for users. A deb=
it that would take the balance below zero must fail with <code>INSUFFICIENT=
_FUNDS</code>.</p></div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"how-fresh-must-the-balance-be" data-toc=3D"5.=
 How fresh must the balance be?"><span class=3D"qn">Q5</span>How fresh must=
 the balance be?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</sp=
an><p>Tells us which reads can come from replicas or a cache and which must=
 hit the primary.</p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Inter=
viewer answer</span><p>The balance used to approve a debit must be exact. T=
he balance shown on the home screen and the history list may lag by a secon=
d or two.</p></div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"will-clients-retry-requests-what-delivery-gua=
rantee-do-we-need" data-toc=3D"6. Will clients retry requests? What deliver=
y =E2=80=A6"><span class=3D"qn">Q6</span>Will clients retry requests? What =
delivery guarantee do we need?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-=
l">Why ask</span><p>Mobile networks drop responses. If "send =E2=82=B9500" =
times out, the app retries, and without protection Ravi gets =E2=82=B91,000=
. <span class=3D"pill our">our addition</span></p></div><div class=3D"qa an=
s"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>Yes, clients and our ow=
n services retry. Every money movement must take effect <strong>exactly onc=
e</strong>: at-least-once delivery plus idempotency.</p></div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"how-do-top-ups-and-withdrawals-reach-the-outs=
ide-world" data-toc=3D"7. How do top-ups and withdrawals reach the =E2=80=
=A6"><span class=3D"qn">Q7</span>How do top-ups and withdrawals reach the o=
utside world?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span>=
<p>External systems can't join our database transaction. A gateway can char=
ge the card and then fail to tell us; a bank transfer can time out and stil=
l succeed later.</p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interv=
iewer answer</span><p>Top-ups go through a payment gateway (PSP) that calls=
 us back with a signed webhook. Withdrawals go through a bank payout API (I=
MPS / NEFT) that is asynchronous and can stay "pending" for hours.</p></div=
></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"are-there-balance-and-transaction-limits" dat=
a-toc=3D"8. Are there balance and transaction limits?"><span class=3D"qn">Q=
8</span>Are there balance and transaction limits?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"=
><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>Limits must be checked in the same t=
ransaction as the money movement, or two concurrent top-ups can both pass t=
he check. <span class=3D"pill our">our addition</span></p></div><div class=
=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>Yes, by KYC le=
vel (RBI-style prepaid-wallet rules): e.g. a minimum-KYC wallet can hold at=
 most =E2=82=B910,000; a full-KYC wallet up to =E2=82=B92,00,000. Plus dail=
y and monthly outflow limits.</p></div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"are-there-very-hot-wallets" data-toc=3D"9. Ar=
e there very hot wallets?"><span class=3D"qn">Q9</span>Are there very hot w=
allets?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>A b=
ig merchant can receive thousands of payments a second. All of them update =
one balance row, which can become the bottleneck.</p></div><div class=3D"qa=
 ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>Yes. Large merchants=
 (food delivery, ticketing) can receive up to ~2,000 payments/s during a sa=
le.</p></div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"what-audit-and-reconciliation-do-we-need" dat=
a-toc=3D"10. What audit and reconciliation do we need?"><span class=3D"qn">=
Q10</span>What audit and reconciliation do we need?</h3><div class=3D"qa wh=
y"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</span><p>Financial regulators want full his=
tory; finance wants our numbers to match the gateway's and the bank's settl=
ement files every day.</p></div><div class=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">=
Interviewer answer</span><p>Keep an immutable history of every movement for=
 10 years. Reconcile daily with the payment gateways and banks. Corrections=
 are new entries, never edits.</p></div></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"single-region-or-multi-region" data-toc=3D"11=
. Single region or multi-region?"><span class=3D"qn">Q11</span>Single regio=
n or multi-region?</h3><div class=3D"qa why"><span class=3D"qa-l">Why ask</=
span><p>Active-active writes across regions make "no double spend" much har=
der (two regions could both approve the last =E2=82=B9500).</p></div><div c=
lass=3D"qa ans"><span class=3D"qa-l">Interviewer answer</span><p>One active=
 region with a hot standby region for disaster recovery. Committed money mu=
st not be lost on failover (RPO =3D 0 within the region).</p></div></div>
</div></section>

<section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"requirements-gathering" data-toc=3D"2. Req=
uirements"><span class=3D"sec-n">2.</span> Requirements Gathering</h2>
<p class=3D"lead">Before diving into the design, let's outline the function=
al and non-functional requirements.</p>
<h3 id=3D"functional-requirements" data-toc=3D"Functional Requirements">Fun=
ctional Requirements</h3>
<div class=3D"cards grid2">
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"wallet-balance"><span class=3D"qn">1</span>Wa=
llet & Balance</h3><p>Create a wallet per user (one per user, many per merc=
hant). Show the <strong>available</strong> balance (what can be spent) and =
amounts <strong>on hold</strong> (e.g. a withdrawal in progress).</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"add-money-credit"><span class=3D"qn">2</span>=
Add Money (Credit)</h3><p>Top up from card, UPI or netbanking through a pay=
ment gateway. Credit the wallet only after the gateway confirms the payment=
.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"pay-send-debit-credit"><span class=3D"qn">3</=
span>Pay & Send (Debit + Credit)</h3><p>Pay a merchant or send to another u=
ser. The sender is debited and the receiver credited <strong>atomically</st=
rong>: both happen or neither does.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"withdraw-debit"><span class=3D"qn">4</span>Wi=
thdraw (Debit)</h3><p>Move money from the wallet to a linked bank account. =
Funds are reserved immediately and the payout completes asynchronously.</p>=
</div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"refunds-reversals"><span class=3D"qn">5</span=
>Refunds & Reversals</h3><p>A merchant can refund a payment fully or partly=
 (never more than was paid). Failed withdrawals and failed transfers are re=
versed automatically.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"history-statements"><span class=3D"qn">6</spa=
n>History & Statements</h3><p>Paginated transaction history and monthly sta=
tements, with each entry showing the balance after it.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"limits-kyc"><span class=3D"qn">7</span>Limits=
 & KYC</h3><p>Enforce balance caps and daily / monthly limits by KYC level.=
 Freeze a wallet on fraud signals.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"notifications"><span class=3D"qn">8</span>Not=
ifications</h3><p>SMS / push for every credit and debit (via the Notificati=
on Service from our earlier design).</p></div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"non-functional-requirements" data-toc=3D"Non-Functional Requireme=
nts">Non-Functional Requirements</h3>
<div class=3D"cards grid2">
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"correctness-above-all"><span class=3D"qn">1</=
span>Correctness Above All</h3><p>No lost money, no double spend, no negati=
ve balance, no double credit. For every transaction, total debits =3D total=
 credits. This is the requirement that drives the whole design.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"exactly-once-effect"><span class=3D"qn">2</sp=
an>Exactly-Once Effect</h3><p>Any request can be retried any number of time=
s (client retries, service retries, duplicate webhooks) and money moves onc=
e.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"strong-consistency-for-money"><span class=3D"=
qn">3</span>Strong Consistency for Money</h3><p>The balance check and the d=
ebit happen in one ACID transaction. Display-only reads may be slightly sta=
le.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"high-availability"><span class=3D"qn">4</span=
>High Availability</h3><p>99.99% for pay / send. If we must choose, we reje=
ct a payment rather than risk a wrong balance.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"low-latency"><span class=3D"qn">5</span>Low L=
atency</h3><p>Wallet-to-wallet pay / send p99 under 300 ms. Top-ups and wit=
hdrawals depend on external systems and are asynchronous.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"durability-auditability"><span class=3D"qn">6=
</span>Durability & Auditability</h3><p>A committed transaction survives an=
y single machine failure (RPO =3D 0). Ledger history is append-only and kep=
t 10 years.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"scalability"><span class=3D"qn">7</span>Scala=
bility</h3><p>Handle ~6K money movements/s at peak and grow by adding shard=
s.</p></div>
<div class=3D"card"><h3 id=3D"security"><span class=3D"qn">8</span>Security=
</h3><p>Strong authentication for debits, encrypted PII and bank details, s=
igned webhooks, least-privilege access to the ledger.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"scale-estimation" data-toc=3D"3. Scale Est=
imation"><span class=3D"sec-n">3.</span> Scale Estimation</h2>
<p class=3D"ask">=E2=80=9CWould you like me to cover back-of-the-envelope c=
alculations, or focus on the design?=E2=80=9D</p>
<h4>Assumptions</h4>
<ul class=3D"bl"><li><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Wallets:</strong> 100M,=
 of which 20M are active daily.</p></div></li><li><div class=3D"li-b"><p><s=
trong>Money movements:</strong> 50M a day (payments, transfers, top-ups, wi=
thdrawals, refunds).</p></div></li><li><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Peak:=
</strong> 3=C3=97 the average at daily peak, 10=C3=97 during a festival sal=
e.</p></div></li><li><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Reads:</strong> about 1=
0 balance / history reads per money movement.</p></div></li></ul>
<h4>Traffic</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">Money movements    50M / day =C3=B7 86,400 s                        =
=E2=89=88 580 / s
  daily peak       =C3=97 3                                          =E2=89=
=88 1.7K / s
  sale peak        =C3=97 10                                         =E2=89=
=88 6K / s
Reads              =C3=97 10 of writes                               =E2=89=
=88 6K / s avg, 60K / s at sale peak
Row writes         per movement: 1 transaction + 2 ledger entries + 2 balan=
ce updates
                   + 1 idempotency row + 1 outbox row           =E2=89=88 7=
 rows
                   6K / s =C3=97 7                                   =E2=89=
=88 42K row writes / s at peak</code></pre></div>
<h4>Storage</h4>
<div class=3D"code"><div class=3D"code-bar"><span class=3D"dots"><i></i><i>=
</i><i></i></span><span class=3D"lang">Text</span></div><pre><code class=3D=
"hljs">Wallets            100M =C3=97 ~200 B                               =
 =E2=89=88 20 GB
Ledger entries     50M =C3=97 2 entries =C3=97 ~250 B (row + indexes)     =
=E2=89=88 25 GB / day
Transactions       50M =C3=97 ~400 B                                 =E2=89=
=88 20 GB / day
Total              =E2=89=88 45 GB / day                                =E2=
=89=88 16 TB / year
Kept 10 years      =E2=89=88 160 TB  =E2=86=92 last ~13 months hot in MySQL=
 (~18 TB), older partitions archived</code></pre></div>
<h4>Key insights</h4>
<ol class=3D"nl"><li><span class=3D"n">1</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><stro=
ng>Writes are the hard part, not reads:</strong> 42K row writes/s at peak w=
ith fsync on every commit is too much for one MySQL primary, and 18 TB of h=
ot data is too big for one box. We <strong>shard by <code>wallet_id</code><=
/strong> (16 shards =E2=89=88 600 transactions/s and ~1.1 TB per shard).</p=
></div></li><li><span class=3D"n">2</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Sh=
arding creates the real problem:</strong> with 16 shards, ~15 of every 16 P=
2P transfers have sender and receiver on <em>different</em> databases. That=
 is where two-phase commit vs saga comes in (Deep Dive 4).</p></div></li><l=
i><span class=3D"n">3</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Hot rows:</stron=
g> one wallet row can take only a few hundred to ~1,000 updates/s (each upd=
ate holds the row lock until commit + fsync). A merchant receiving 2,000 pa=
yments/s needs special handling (Deep Dive 8).</p></div></li><li><span clas=
s=3D"n">4</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Reads are cheap:</strong> a =
balance read is a primary-key lookup; history comes from a separate read mo=
del.</p></div></li></ol>
</section>

<section class=3D"sec"><h2 id=3D"api-design" data-toc=3D"4. API Design"><sp=
