<ol class=3D"nl"><li><span class=3D"n">1</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><stro=
ng>Lock in a global order:</strong> always lock the two wallets in ascendin=
g <code>wallet_id</code> (<code>SELECT =E2=80=A6 WHERE wallet_id IN (a, b) =
ORDER BY wallet_id FOR UPDATE</code>). Both transactions now want the lower=
 id first, so one simply waits for the other. No cycle is possible.</p></di=
v></li><li><span class=3D"n">2</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Still r=
etry on 1213 and 1205</strong> (deadlock, lock-wait timeout): InnoDB rolls =
back the whole transaction, and with the idempotency key inside it, a retry=
 is safe. Retry 2=E2=80=933 times with jitter.</p></div></li><li><span clas=
s=3D"n">3</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Keep transactions tiny:</str=
ong> no HTTP calls, no Kafka sends, no user think-time inside a transaction=
. The gateway call happens <em>before</em> (create order) or <em>after</em>=
 (webhook), never while a wallet row is locked.</p></div></li><li><span cla=
ss=3D"n">4</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Lower <code>innodb_lock_wai=
t_timeout</code></strong> from the default 50 s to ~2 s for the wallet serv=
ice, so a stuck lock fails fast instead of piling up requests.</p></div></l=
i></ol>
<div class=3D"callout interview"><div class=3D"ct">=F0=9F=92=AC Interview l=
ine</div><p>"Two-wallet transactions lock rows in wallet-id order, which ru=
les out lock cycles; any remaining deadlock or lock timeout just rolls back=
 and is retried, which is safe because the idempotency key is part of the s=
ame transaction."</p></div>
<h3 id=3D"deep-dive-4-asha-and-ravi-are-on-different-shards-do-we-need-two-=
phase-commit" class=3D"dd" data-toc=3D"Deep Dive 4: Cross-shard: 2PC vs sag=
a"><span class=3D"dd-n">Deep Dive 4</span><span class=3D"dd-q">Asha and Rav=
i are on different shards. Do we need two-phase commit?</span></h3>
<p>A transfer must debit shard 3 and credit shard 9 <strong>all-or-nothing<=
/strong>. A single MySQL transaction can't span two servers. There are two =
families of answers: make the two databases commit together (<strong>2PC</s=
trong>), or commit them one after another and guarantee the rest finishes o=
r is undone (<strong>saga</strong>).</p>

<h4 class=3D"approach"><span class=3D"ap-tag">Approach 1</span>Two-phase co=
mmit (XA transactions)</h4>
<p>A <strong>coordinator</strong> (our Transfer Service) runs a branch on e=
ach shard and then asks both to commit together:</p>
<ol class=3D"nl"><li><span class=3D"n">1</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><stro=
ng>Phase 1 =E2=80=94 Prepare (voting):</strong> each shard does its work, w=
rites it durably to its redo log, keeps its row locks, and votes YES ("I ca=
n definitely commit") or NO.</p></div></li><li><span class=3D"n">2</span><d=
iv class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Decision:</strong> if every vote is YES, the c=
oordinator durably writes <code>COMMIT</code> to its own log. <strong>This =
write is the commit point.</strong> Any NO or timeout =E2=86=92 <code>ABORT=
</code>.</p></div></li><li><span class=3D"n">3</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p=
><strong>Phase 2 =E2=80=94 Commit / rollback:</strong> the coordinator tell=
s every shard the decision; each commits (or rolls back) and releases its l=
ocks. Retried until every shard acknowledges.</p></div></li></ol>
