<h4>What happens when things fail</h4>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>What happens</th><=
th>Who is stuck?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A shard can't do its part =
(insufficient funds, constraint)</td><td>it votes NO =E2=86=92 coordinator =
aborts =E2=86=92 <code>XA ROLLBACK</code> on all</td><td>nobody</td></tr><t=
r><td>A shard crashes <strong>before</strong> PREPARE</td><td>its branch is=
 lost; coordinator times out =E2=86=92 abort</td><td>nobody</td></tr><tr><t=
d>A shard crashes <strong>after</strong> PREPARE</td><td>on restart, the br=
anch is still PREPARED (<code>XA RECOVER</code>), holding its locks, waitin=
g for the decision</td><td>that wallet, until the coordinator answers</td><=
/tr><tr><td>Coordinator crashes <strong>before</strong> logging the decisio=
n</td><td>recovery finds no decision =E2=86=92 <strong>presumed abort</stro=
ng> =E2=86=92 <code>XA ROLLBACK</code> prepared branches</td><td>both walle=
ts, until recovery runs</td></tr><tr><td>Coordinator crashes <strong>after<=
/strong> logging COMMIT</td><td>recovery re-sends <code>XA COMMIT</code> to=
 every branch until all acknowledge</td><td>both wallets, until recovery ru=
ns</td></tr><tr class=3D"hl"><td>Network partition between coordinator and =
a prepared shard</td><td>the shard <strong>cannot decide alone</strong> (th=
e other shard may already have committed); it must wait</td><td>that wallet=
, for as long as the partition lasts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<ol class=3D"nl"><li class=3D"pro"><span class=3D"n">1</span><div class=3D"=
li-b"><p><strong>Pros:</strong> true atomicity <em>and</em> isolation: nobo=
dy ever sees Asha debited without Ravi credited. Works with ordinary SQL on=
 each shard.</p></div></li><li class=3D"con"><span class=3D"n">2</span><div=
 class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Cons:</strong> <strong>blocking:</strong> a prep=
ared branch holds row locks across several network round trips and a log fs=
ync, and indefinitely if the coordinator is down. Asha's next payment waits=
 behind it. Latency =3D slowest shard + 2 round trips + 2 fsyncs. The coord=
inator log becomes critical infrastructure, and XA has operational sharp ed=
ges (prepared transactions that someone must resolve by hand).</p></div></l=
i></ol>

<h4 class=3D"approach"><span class=3D"ap-tag">Approach 2</span>Saga: local =
transactions + clearing account + compensation</h4>
<p>Flow 2 above. Each step is a normal local transaction that commits immed=
iately, so no lock is ever held across shards:</p>
<div class=3D"tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Shard</th><th>Ledger =
entries</th><th>transfers.state</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>T1 debit</t=
d><td>3</td><td>DEBIT Asha 500 =C2=B7 CREDIT transfer_clearing@3 500</td><t=
d><code>DEBITED</code></td></tr><tr><td>T2 credit</td><td>9</td><td>DEBIT t=
ransfer_clearing@9 500 =C2=B7 CREDIT Ravi 500 (+ <code>applied_transfers</c=
ode> row)</td><td>=E2=80=94</td></tr><tr><td>T3 finish</td><td>3</td><td>=
=E2=80=94</td><td><code>COMPLETED</code></td></tr><tr><td>T2=E2=80=B2 compe=
nsate (only if T2 can never succeed)</td><td>3</td><td>DEBIT transfer_clear=
ing@3 500 =C2=B7 CREDIT Asha 500</td><td><code>REVERSED</code></td></tr></t=
body></table></div>
<ol class=3D"nl"><li><span class=3D"n">1</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><stro=
ng>Every step balances on its own shard,</strong> and across the system <co=
de>transfer_clearing@3</code> (+500) and <code>transfer_clearing@9</code> (=
=E2=88=92500) cancel out once both steps are done. The sum of all clearing =
accounts =3D money currently in flight. Reconciliation checks it.</p></div>=
</li><li><span class=3D"n">2</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Every ste=
p is idempotent:</strong> T1 by the idempotency key, T2 by <code>applied_tr=
ansfers.txn_id</code> (primary key), T3 and T2=E2=80=B2 by conditional stat=
e transitions (<code>WHERE state =3D 'DEBITED'</code>).</p></div></li><li><=
span class=3D"n">3</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Recovery is "retry =
forward":</strong> a worker picks <code>transfers</code> rows stuck in <cod=
e>DEBITED</code> past <code>next_retry_at</code> and re-runs T2 then T3. Co=
mpensation only on a <em>permanent</em> error (receiver closed / frozen / o=
ver cap), never on a timeout, because T2 might have committed.</p></div></l=
i><li><span class=3D"n">4</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Debit first,=
 credit second:</strong> if we credited first and the debit failed, Ravi co=
uld spend money that never arrived. Debiting first means the worst intermed=
iate state is "money in clearing", which is safe.</p></div></li></ol>
<ol class=3D"nl"><li class=3D"pro"><span class=3D"n">1</span><div class=3D"=
li-b"><p><strong>Pros:</strong> no cross-shard locks, no blocking, each ste=
p is fast (a few ms); a shard outage delays the credit but never freezes th=
e sender's wallet; failures are visible as rows in a table.</p></div></li><=
li class=3D"con"><span class=3D"n">2</span><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>C=
ons:</strong> <strong>no isolation:</strong> for ~10=E2=80=93100 ms Asha is=
 debited and Ravi not yet credited (he may see it a moment later); compensa=
tion logic must be written and tested; needs a recovery worker and monitori=
ng of stuck transfers.</p></div></li></ol>
<div class=3D"callout tip"><div class=3D"ct">Variant: TCC (Try=E2=80=93Conf=
irm=E2=80=93Cancel)</div><p>Instead of debiting into a clearing account, <s=
trong>Try</strong> puts a hold on Asha (<code>held_minor +=3D 500</code>), =
<strong>Confirm</strong> turns the hold into a debit and credits Ravi, <str=
ong>Cancel</strong> releases the hold. Same saga structure; it just keeps t=
he money visibly "on hold" in Asha's wallet while in flight.</p></div>

<h4 class=3D"approach"><span class=3D"ap-tag">Approach 3</span>Avoid the cr=
oss-shard case</h4>
<ul class=3D"bl"><li><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Co-locate</strong> wall=
ets that always transact together (a merchant's sub-wallets, a family walle=
t group) on one shard.</p></div></li><li><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>Use=
 a distributed SQL database</strong> (Spanner, CockroachDB, TiDB, YugabyteD=
B). They still run 2PC internally, but each participant and the transaction=
 record are replicated with Paxos / Raft, so a crashed coordinator is repla=
ced automatically and the blocking problem mostly disappears, at the cost o=
f higher write latency and a different operational model.</p></div></li><li=
><div class=3D"li-b"><p><strong>For random P2P you can't avoid it:</strong>=
 with 16 shards, ~94% of transfers cross shards.</p></div></li></ul>

<div class=3D"tbl"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>2PC (XA)</th><th>Saga (ou=
r choice)</th><th>Distributed SQL</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Atomic</t=
d><td>=E2=9C=85</td><td>=E2=9C=85 eventually (retry forward or compensate)<=
/td><td>=E2=9C=85</td></tr><tr><td>Isolation (no in-between state visible)<=
/td><td>=E2=9C=85</td><td>=E2=9D=8C money visibly "in transit"</td><td>=E2=
=9C=85</td></tr><tr><td>Locks held across shards</td><td>yes, until the dec=
ision</td><td>never</td><td>yes, but short and replicated</td></tr><tr><td>=
Coordinator failure</td><td>blocks prepared rows</td><td>worker resumes fro=
m the transfers table</td><td>automatic failover</td></tr><tr><td>Latency</=
td><td>2 round trips + 2 fsyncs + slowest shard</td><td>T1 + T2 =E2=89=88 t=
wo local commits</td><td>consensus write latency</td></tr><tr><td>Complexit=
y lives in</td><td>XA recovery ops</td><td>compensation + idempotency code<=
/td><td>the database</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class=3D"callout "><div class=3D"ct">=E2=9C=85 Our choice</div><p>A <s=
trong>saga with a clearing account</strong> for cross-shard transfers, and =
a plain local transaction when both wallets share a shard. 2PC is correct a=
nd is the right answer when you truly need isolation across a few internal =
databases at modest volume (for example moving money between two internal l=
edgers that auditors require to change together). For a high-volume wallet =
where any wallet row may be hot, holding locks across a network round trip =
and blocking on coordinator failure is the wrong trade.</p></div>
<div class=3D"callout interview"><div class=3D"ct">=F0=9F=92=AC Interview l=
ine</div><p>"Same-shard transfers are one ACID transaction. Cross-shard I u=
se a saga: debit the sender into a per-shard clearing account, credit the r=
eceiver idempotently on the other shard, and drive it with a durable state =
machine that retries forward and compensates only on permanent failure. I'd=
 use 2PC only where I need isolation across a couple of internal databases =
and can live with blocking if the coordinator dies."</p></div>
